.TH CCM 1 "October 2026" "ccm 0.0.10" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccm \- monitor CPU, GPU, memory, and temperature of multiple hosts via SSH or talosctl
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccm
[\fB\-n\fR \fISECONDS\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-hostfile\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-hosts\fR \fIHOST\fR[,\fIHOST\fR...]]
[\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-threads\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ccm
is an ncurses-based command line tool that monitors CPU utilization, GPU load,
memory usage, and CPU temperature of multiple hosts over SSH, displaying real-time
horizontal bar graphs. Both Linux and FreeBSD hosts are supported as
monitoring targets. Talos Linux nodes, which have no SSH, are monitored through
.BR talosctl (1)
instead.
.PP
Hosts are queried in parallel using multiple SSH or
.B talosctl
connections. The display
shows the hostname, CPU load, memory usage, and statistics columns
(user/system/nice/idle CPU percentages and temperature). Press
.B q
or
.B ESC
to quit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-interval \fISECONDS\fR
Update interval in seconds. Defaults to 0.5.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-hostfile \fIFILE\fR
Use the specified hosts file. Defaults to
.I hosts.txt
with fallbacks to
.I ~/.ccmrc
and
.I /etc/ccm.conf
.
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-hosts \fIHOST\fR[,\fIHOST\fR...]
Monitor the given comma-separated list of hosts. May be combined with a host
file.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-threads \fIN\fR
Number of parallel SSH or
.B talosctl
threads. Defaults to 4, clamped to the range 1\(en100.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.SH HOSTS FILE FORMAT
The hosts file contains one hostname per line. Lines starting with
.B #
are ignored as comments.
.PP
A plain hostname is queried over SSH. A hostname prefixed with
.B talos:
is a Talos Linux node and is queried with
.B talosctl
instead. The prefix is accepted in the hosts file and in the
.B \-H
list alike:
.PP
.nf
.RS
node1
talos:192.168.1.10
.RE
.fi
.SH GPU LOAD
On Linux and Talos Linux hosts with an AMD GPU (the
.B amdgpu
driver, including integrated GPUs),
.B ccm
shows the GPU load as a third bar next to CPU and memory. The value is read from
.IR /sys/class/drm/card*/device/gpu_busy_percent .
.PP
The bar is shown as soon as one host reports a GPU and the terminal is at least
90 columns wide; otherwise the display is unchanged. Hosts without a supported
GPU show
.B n/a
in the bar. NVIDIA and Intel GPUs and FreeBSD hosts are not supported.
.SH TALOS LINUX NODES
Talos Linux has no shell or SSH, so
.B ccm
reads
.IR /proc/stat ,
.I /proc/meminfo
and the CPU temperature sensor under
.I /sys
with
.BI "talosctl \-\-nodes=" HOST " read " FILE .
CPU percentages are derived from the difference between two samples of the
cumulative counters, so the first reading of a node takes a fraction of a second
longer than later ones. A temperature of
.B ---
is shown if the node has no CPU sensor.
.PP
.B talosctl
uses the talosconfig named by the
.B TALOSCONFIG
environment variable, or
.I ~/.talos/config
if it is unset, and the endpoints of its current context. Name nodes exactly as
.B talosctl get members
lists them (or by IP), because a node's certificate is only valid for its own
Talos hostname and addresses. To see why a node is shown as failed, run
.BI "talosctl \-\-nodes=" HOST " read /proc/stat"
yourself. Each call is stopped
after 3 seconds, so an unreachable node is shown as failed without blocking the
display.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I hosts.txt
Default hosts file, read from the current working directory.
.TP
.I ~/.ccmrc
Per-user hosts file, used if
.I hosts.txt
is not present.
.TP
.I /etc/ccm.conf
System-wide hosts file, used as a last fallback.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
SSH access to the monitored hosts with key-based authentication
.B (BatchMode=yes)
is required; no password prompt can be answered. Hosts prefixed with
.B talos:
instead require
.B talosctl
in
.B PATH
and a working talosconfig.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (no hosts specified, terminal too small, or privilege
drop failed).
